pydebuprep (0.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reports carry a machine ID, so a portal can tell apart two machines that
    report the same host name: not /etc/machine-id itself, but the ID systemd
    derives from it for pydebuprep (systemd-id128 machine-id -a
    e44561703c184c26a2c15fb541fc276a prints the same). Left out when the host
    has no machine ID.
  * Upgrade a portal before its hosts: one built on pydebuprep 0.7.0 or older
    refuses reports that carry a machine ID (pydebuprep-portal 0.3.0 takes
    both).
  * New check nginx: includes that point at missing files (dangling
    sites-enabled and modules-enabled links), load_module files that are
    gone, and unix: sockets in *_pass and upstream server lines that don't
    exist, naming the sockets next to them (php7.4-fpm.sock after PHP became
    8.2). Read from the files; nginx -t isn't used.
  * New check apache: the same for Include, IncludeOptional, LoadModule and
    unix: sockets (PHP-FPM SetHandler, ProxyPass). apachectl -t isn't used.
  * New check libvirt: domains set to start at boot that aren't running, and
    disk images, devices or emulators that no longer exist. Read from
    libvirt's files; virsh isn't used.
  * The documentation site is built with Zensical.

 -- Jostein Elvaker Haande <jehaande@gmail.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 13:30:00 +0100

pydebuprep (0.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Regular expressions from a config (logs.ignore and the journal-errors and
    dmesg-errors ignore options) are limited: at most 100 patterns of at most
    200 characters, and none that repeats a part which itself repeats without
    bound, the shape behind catastrophic backtracking. A config from a portal
    could otherwise make every host's run pin a CPU.
  * Breaking: configs with such patterns, or more than 100 of them, are now
    refused.
  * Files are checked again after they are opened, so a path swapped for a
    FIFO or a device in between is refused.
  * A symlinked token file says so, and how to fix it.
  * The published report schema requires references to be http(s) links.
  * The package ships py.typed for type checkers.

 -- Jostein Elvaker Haande <jehaande@gmail.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 16:34:01 +0100

pydebuprep (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security hardening from an external review:
    - files are read only when they are regular files (never a FIFO or a
      device), without following a final symlink, at most 16 MiB at a time;
    - the portal token file must be a regular file, not a symlink;
    - report files are created exclusively, mode 0600, never through a
      symlink;
    - a config fetched from a URL may no longer set python-venvs'
      search_paths or docker's http_probes; python-venvs' max_depth is at
      most 20;
    - MARIADB*, APT_* and DOCKER_* environment variables are not passed to
      the commands pydebuprep runs;
    - control characters are stripped from every field of rendered reports,
      from portal replies and from baseline warnings;
    - finding references must be http(s) URLs, both in saved reports and
      when rendered as links.
  * Breaking: configs with max_depth above 20, fetched configs setting
    search_paths or http_probes, and saved reports with non-URL references
    are now refused.

 -- Jostein Elvaker Haande <jehaande@gmail.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:06:36 +0100

pydebuprep (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Optional upload to a pydebuprep portal: with `upload: url:` in the
    config file, each report is sent as JSON over HTTPS with a token read
    from /etc/pydebuprep/token (mode 0600) or PYDEBUPREP_TOKEN. A failed
    upload never fails the report; the JSON is kept in
    /var/lib/pydebuprep/pending/ for later.
  * New command `pydebuprep upload` resends kept reports, or report files
    saved with --format json or yaml.
  * --config accepts an HTTPS URL. A copy of the fetched config is saved
    in /var/lib/pydebuprep/configs/ and used when the portal can't be
    reached; the report's header then says the config may not be the
    latest. A config from a URL can't name files to write or open, and
    its upload URL must be on the same server.
  * New options --ca-file, --token-file and --allow-http (test setups
    only). Certificates are checked against the system's CAs; redirects
    are never followed.
  * JSON Schemas of the report and config formats, published at
    https://pydebuprep.app/schema/.
  * The package creates /var/lib/pydebuprep/pending and
    /var/lib/pydebuprep/configs (mode 0700); the read-only audit now
    covers report runs that upload and use a config from a URL.
  * zsh completion describes --no-todo as turning --todo off.

 -- Jostein Elvaker Haande <jehaande@gmail.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:04:48 +0100

pydebuprep (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New check systemd-critical-services: units a host can't do without
    (ssh, cron and journald where installed, plus configured ones) must be
    present, running and enabled; renamed, masked, disabled, failed and
    never-started units are told apart, and changes since the baseline
    are reported.
  * apt-sources: missing Signed-By keyrings, keys in the legacy
    /etc/apt/trusted.gpg, missing security archive, repositories with no
    downloaded metadata or metadata for a different release, and InRelease
    signatures verified again against the keys apt would use (sqv or gpgv,
    with keys copied into a private scratch directory under /tmp that is
    removed before the check returns).
  * New command `pydebuprep convert` renders a saved JSON or YAML report in
    another format.
  * New option --todo adds a checklist of what needs fixing to the end of a
    report, in every format.
  * Opt-in readiness probes, off by default: PostgreSQL (pg_isready and a
    SELECT 1 per online cluster), MariaDB/MySQL (ping and a query) and
    Docker (published TCP ports and localhost HTTP URLs). Docker health
    states (unhealthy, restarting, starting) are now separate findings.
  * apt-upgradable separates pending security updates, packages kept back,
    updates deferred by phasing, and other upgrades (apt-get -s upgrade).
  * dpkg-audit reports packages unpacked but not configured, needing
    reinstalling, or waiting for triggers, from dpkg's own states.
  * obsolete-packages groups packages from third-party repositories by
    vendor and reports packages whose origin changed since the baseline;
    package indexes are streamed, so memory use stays low.
  * python-venvs reports compiled modules needing libraries the linker
    can't find (read from their ELF headers, never by running ldd) and
    scripts whose interpreter doesn't exist.
  * Documentation site at https://pydebuprep.app/ with a page per check.

 -- Jostein Elvaker Haande <jehaande@gmail.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 08:27:17 +0100

